# Third-party actions that are NOT pinned to a commit SHA.
#
# Pinning is the default (B5-L1): a mutable tag like `@v2` means the publisher
# can change the code that runs in a job holding `id-token: write`. Only
# `NuGet/login` remains on a tag, because its commit SHA could not be resolved
# when the pin was introduced (no network access to the registry).
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------- format ----
#
# One entry per line, TAB separated, five fields:
#
#   1. the `uses:` value, byte-identical to the workflow  (parsed by the lint)
#   2. review period: monthly | quarterly | yearly
#   3. last reviewed: never | YYYY-MM-DD
#   4. the command that resolves the ref to a commit SHA
#   5. a note: what is known, and what is still open
#
# `workflow_lint.py` matches field 1 exactly and rejects a malformed line, so a
# tag cannot be parked here without a review period and a resolve command
# attached (R6). Blank lines and lines starting with `#` are ignored.
#
# -------------------------------------------------------- quarterly review --
#
# Every entry is re-checked once a period. `scripts/tests/action_pin_review.py`
# reads this file, prints the resolve command for each entry, and - when run with
# `--resolve` on a machine that has network and `gh`/`git` - runs it, prints the
# commit SHA the ref points at today and the date the review is next due. It never
# edits the file: the reviewer pastes the SHA into field 5 and today's date into
# field 3.
#
#     python3 scripts/tests/action_pin_review.py            # plan only, no network
#     python3 scripts/tests/action_pin_review.py --resolve  # needs network + gh
#
# The same commands and their expected output are in docs/releasing.md
# ("Review the unpinned actions").
#
# VERIFICATION STATE (read this before trusting the note below):
#
#   * The review itself WAS run, once, on 2026-11-07 from the machine that wrote
#     this file, against the live registry (`gh api`, which is authenticated).
#     The commit sha in the note is therefore a real observation, not a guess.
#   * The workflow was NOT run on GitHub: nothing here proves that
#     `NuGet/login@<sha>` is accepted by the runner, that the OIDC exchange still
#     succeeds, or that the release jobs pass. The pin below was deliberately NOT
#     applied for exactly that reason - changing it is a release-day edit that
#     wants a green CI run behind it.
#
# The trap worth remembering: `gh api .../git/ref/tags/v1` answers with the
# ANNOTATED TAG OBJECT's sha, which is not the commit a workflow pin uses. Only
# `gh api .../commits/v1` (or the `^{}` form of `git ls-remote`) peels to the
# commit. The review script uses the peeling form for this reason.
#
# uses                        period     last reviewed  resolve command                                                    note
NuGet/login@v1	quarterly	2026-11-07	gh api repos/NuGet/login/commits/v1 --jq .sha	v1 is an ANNOTATED tag -> commit 8d196754b4036150537f80ac539e15c2f1028841 (tag object ebc737b6fc418a6ca0073cf116ec8dc156d8b81e, tagger 2026-04-24, commit 'Updated to node24' 2026-04-22), observed 2026-11-07 via gh api on the authoring machine. FOLLOW-UP: the workflow still uses @v1 and was NOT changed - re-pin to the commit sha in a release-day PR with a green CI run.
